<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Estimating a power of a number just above 1</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Find the first four terms of the expansion of \((1 + x)^{@n@}\) in ascending powers of \(x\).</p><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Use your answer to (a) to estimate \({@Nd@}^{@n@}\). Give your answer correct to 4 decimal places.</p><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>When \(x\) is small, each term is much smaller than the one before, so a few terms give the value to the accuracy you need.</p><p>(a) The row for power \({@n@}\) starts 1, \({@n@}\), \({@binomial(n,2)@}\), \({@binomial(n,3)@}\), so \((1 + x)^{@n@} = {@tad@} + \cdots\)</p><p>(b) \({@Nd@} = 1 + {@dd@}\), so put \(x = {@dd@}\). The four terms are \({@tv[1]@}\), \({@tv[2]@}\), \({@tv[3]@}\) and \({@tv[4]@}\). Their sum is \({@S4d@}\), so \({@Nd@}^{@n@} \approx {@R4@}\) to 4 decimal places.</p><p>The next term is \({@nxt@}\), too small to change the fourth decimal place. A calculator gives \({@Nd@}^{@n@} = {@calc@}\ldots\), which also rounds to \({@R4@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[validate_x(ex) := if every(lambda([v], is(v=x)), listofvars(ex)) then "" else "Use only the letter x.";
dec(v) := block([k:0], while k<10 and not(integerp(ev(10^k*v,simp))) do k:k+1, if k=0 then string(v) else dispdp(float(v),k));
pad3(r) := if r<10 then sconcat("00",r) else if r<100 then sconcat("0",r) else sconcat(r);
grp(v) := if v<0 then sconcat("-",grp(-v)) else if v<10000 then sconcat(v) else sconcat(floor(v/1000),"\\,",pad3(mod(v,1000)));
money(v) := block([c], c: floor(100*v+1/2), if mod(c,100)=0 then grp(c/100) else sconcat(grp(floor(c/100)),".",if mod(c,100)<10 then sconcat("0",mod(c,100)) else sconcat(mod(c,100))));
cstr(c) := if integerp(c) then string(abs(c)) else sconcat("\\frac{",num(abs(c)),"}{",denom(abs(c)),"}");
xstr(j) := if j=0 then "" else if j=1 then "x" else sconcat("x^{",j,"}");
tstr(c,j) := sconcat(if j>0 and abs(c)=1 then "" else cstr(c), xstr(j));
ascd(p) := block([cs,s,j,c,first], s:"", first:true, for j:0 thru hipow(expand(p),x) do (c: coeff(expand(p),x,j), if c#0 then (if first then s: sconcat(if c<0 then "-" else "", tstr(c,j)) else s: sconcat(s, if c<0 then " - " else " + ", tstr(c,j)), first:false)), s);
/* Design: use a truncated expansion to estimate a value. Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Core 2, Chapter 5, Exercise A, question 4(a)(b). */
L: [[5,1/100],[6,1/100],[7,1/100],[9,1/100],[4,3/100],[4,4/100],[5,5/100],[6,4/100],[7,2/100],[9,2/100]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
n: L[ix][1];
d: L[ix][2];
nm1: n-1;
ta: sum(binomial(n,r)*x^r,r,0,3);
S4: subst(d,x,ta);
two: 1+n*d;
x10: subst(10*d,x,ta);
prv: sum(binomial(n-1,r)*x^r,r,0,3);
Nd: dispdp(float(1+d),2);
dd: dispdp(float(d),2);
d10: dispdp(float(10*d),1);
tv: makelist(dec(binomial(n,r)*d^r),r,0,3);
S4d: dec(S4);
R4: dispdp(float(floor(10^4*S4+1/2)/10^4),4);
nxt: dec(binomial(n,4)*d^4);
calc: dispdp(float((1+d)^n),8);
tad: ascd(ta);]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
