  <questiondescription format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p id="concept">Binomial Expansions: approximating a power of a number just above 1</p><p id="description">Grade 12 CBE Core 1.2: Find the first four terms of (1 + x)^n and use them to estimate (1 + d)^n to 4 decimal places.</p><p id="edition">Core</p><p id="provenance">This question is adapted from Chapter 5, Section A, Exercise A, question 4 of Cassell, D., Instone, S., Ling, J., Scruton, P., Shilton, S. and West, H., SMP AS/A2 Mathematics: Core 2 for AQA, Cambridge University Press, 2004: new powers and values of x, randomised, with the estimate given to 4 decimal places.</p><p id="citation">INNODEMS CBC Grade 12 Mathematics, first draft by Ben Davies, 2026. Aligned to the KICD Senior School Curriculum Design, Core Mathematics Grade 12 (draft).</p>]]></text></questiondescription>
